WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:18.916
During the day of January 29, the island of Ceylon disappeared from the horizon.

2
00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:25.170
I consulted the map in the gallery and found that we had traveled 7,500 leagues.

3
00:00:25.171 --> 00:00:32.051
When we rose to the surface the next day, there was no land in sight, and we were once again alone on the ocean.

4
00:00:32.052 --> 00:00:37.210
"Where do you think we're headed now?" asked Ned, who had joined me on the platform.

5
00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:41.480
"We're going wherever our captain's fancy takes us," I replied.

6
00:00:41.481 --> 00:00:46.385
"When I looked at our course on the map, we seemed headed toward the Persian Gulf."

7
00:00:46.386 --> 00:00:50.385
"Captain Nemo's fancy won't take us very far," said Ned.

8
00:00:50.386 --> 00:00:56.710
"The Persian Gulf has no outlet. So if we do enter the Gulf, it won't be long before we have to turn around!"

9
00:00:56.711 --> 00:01:01.464
"Very well, then we'll come out again and maybe we'll visit the Red Sea."

10
00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:05.551
"The Red Sea!" scoffed Ned. "It's a dead end too.

11
00:01:05.552 --> 00:01:12.178
The Suez Canal isn't finished, and even if it were, the captain wouldn't risk taking his submarine through the canal's locks.

12
00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:16.504
No, the Red Sea is not the route that will take us back to Europe."

13
00:01:16.505 --> 00:01:22.766
"Perhaps we will reenter the Indian Ocean and travel down the east coast of Africa until we reach the Cape of Good Hope."

14
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:26.345
"And then where will we go next?" Ned asked.

15
00:01:26.346 --> 00:01:31.345
"Well, then we'll enter a section of the Atlantic Ocean that we're not yet familiar with."

16
00:01:31.346 --> 00:01:37.916
I could tell that Ned was getting tired of this journey under the sea, but I was still an enthusiastic traveler.

17
00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:44.599
"For my sake, I'll be sorry to see the end of a voyage that so few men have had the opportunity to make."

18
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:52.258
"Don't you realize, Professor?" asked Ned. "It will soon be three whole months that we've been imprisoned here?"

19
00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:59.321
"No, Ned, I didn't realize that; in fact, I don't want to realize it. I don't keep track of every day and every hour."

20
00:01:59.322 --> 00:02:04.242
"But when will our imprisonment come to an end?" Ned persisted.

21
00:02:04.243 --> 00:02:07.939
"When it ends. Meanwhile there's nothing we can do about it.

22
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.575
If you tell me someday that we have a good opportunity to escape, then I'll listen to you.

23
00:02:12.576 --> 00:02:16.639
But until then, there's no point in even discussing this anymore."

24
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:23.044
"Waiting and staying silent may be fine for you, but in my opinion, a life in prison is no life at all."

25
00:02:23.045 --> 00:02:26.893
And with that statement, Ned left me alone on the platform.

26
00:02:26.894 --> 00:02:32.298
It turned out that we were headed for the Red Sea, which we reached on February 7.

27
00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:40.719
I don't know why Captain Nemo decided to enter this body of water, but I approved of his decision because we began traveling at a slower speed.

28
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:47.877
This gave me plenty of opportunities to observe life below the sea as well as the ancient towns along its shores.

29
00:02:47.878 --> 00:02:54.599
I spent hours at the gallery windows, gazing at species of plants and animals that I had never seen before.

30
00:02:54.600 --> 00:03:00.679
A couple days later, I was standing on the platform when Captain Nemo came up to take the ship's bearings.

31
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.607
"Well, Professor Aronnax, does the Red Sea please you?

32
00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:11.687
Have you observed the wonders it holds: schools of fish, groupings of sponges, forests of coral?"

33
00:03:11.688 --> 00:03:19.076
"Yes, Captain, and the Nautilus is wonderful for conducting such a study. It is truly a marvelous boat!"

34
00:03:19.077 --> 00:03:21.545
"It is marvelous and indestructible.

35
00:03:21.546 --> 00:03:28.997
Traveling on this submarine, we do not need to fear the terrible storms of the Red Sea, or its currents and sandbanks."

36
00:03:28.998 --> 00:03:34.163
"Your boat is at least a century before its time." I continued with my praise.

37
00:03:34.164 --> 00:03:38.504
"How sad that the secret of this invention will die with its inventor."

38
00:03:38.505 --> 00:03:41.250
Captain Nemo was silent for a few minutes.

39
00:03:41.251 --> 00:03:45.584
"It's a pity I can't take you through the Suez Canal," he said at last.

40
00:03:45.585 --> 00:03:52.560
"But you'll be able to see the long jetty of Port Said the day after tomorrow, when we'll be in the Mediterranean Sea."

41
00:03:52.561 --> 00:03:56.885
"We'll be in the Mediterranean Sea the day after tomorrow?" I exclaimed.

42
00:03:56.886 --> 00:04:02.290
I was surprised, even though by now nothing about the Nautilus should have surprised me.

43
00:04:02.291 --> 00:04:08.472
"We'll have to travel at a rapid speed if we are to round the Cape of Good Hope and reach the Mediterranean by then!"

44
00:04:08.473 --> 00:04:14.869
"Who said that we would sail down the coast of Africa and around the Cape of Good Hope?" Captain Nemo replied.

45
00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:20.060
"Well, unless the Nautilus sails on dry land and passes above the isthmus—"

46
00:04:20.061 --> 00:04:23.498
"Or beneath it, Professor Aronnax," he interrupted me.

47
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.783
"Beneath it?" I echoed.

48
00:04:25.784 --> 00:04:32.870
"That's right," replied Captain Nemo. "There is a subterranean passage that I have named the Arabian Tunnel.

49
00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:36.520
It takes us beneath the Suez and opens into the Mediterranean.

50
00:04:36.521 --> 00:04:44.266
Not only does this passage exist, but I have made use of it several times. Without it, I wouldn't have ventured into the Red Sea."

51
00:04:44.267 --> 00:04:47.115
"But how did you find it?" I asked.

52
00:04:47.116 --> 00:04:50.917
"A combination of luck and reasoning," he replied.

53
00:04:50.918 --> 00:04:58.504
Captain Nemo then explained that he had noticed there were some identical species of fish in the Red Sea and the Mediterranean.

54
00:04:58.505 --> 00:05:05.576
He guessed that the subterranean current ran from the Red Sea to the Mediterranean because the Red Sea was higher.

55
00:05:05.577 --> 00:05:14.235
So he caught a large number of fish in the vicinity of Suez. He put a copper ring through their tails and threw them back into the Red Sea.

56
00:05:14.236 --> 00:05:18.893
A few months later he caught some of these fish in the eastern Mediterranean.

57
00:05:18.894 --> 00:05:22.743
"I then looked for the tunnel with the Nautilus," said the captain.

58
00:05:22.744 --> 00:05:27.481
"I passed through it, and the day after tomorrow, you will too."

59
00:05:27.482 --> 00:05:31.664
On the night of February 11, the Nautilus remained underwater.

60
00:05:31.665 --> 00:05:36.148
According to my calculations, we must have been very near Suez.

61
00:05:36.149 --> 00:05:43.076
"It can't be easy to pass through this tunnel of yours," I said to Captain Nemo as we sat in the gallery after supper.

62
00:05:43.077 --> 00:05:49.878
"No, and for that reason I usually go into the pilothouse and steer the submarine myself. You're welcome to come with me."

63
00:05:49.879 --> 00:05:53.870
We made our way to the pilothouse on top of the submarine.

64
00:05:53.871 --> 00:06:01.473
The cabin was dark, but soon I could see the pilot, a strong man who stood with his hands resting on the large wheel.

65
00:06:01.474 --> 00:06:07.291
Outside, the sea was brightly illuminated by the electric light behind the pilothouse.

66
00:06:07.292 --> 00:06:12.021
"Now," Captain Nemo said. "Let's look for our passageway."

67
00:06:12.022 --> 00:06:18.767
We traveled for almost an hour with Captain Nemo directing the pilot to make small changes in the submarine's course.

68
00:06:18.768 --> 00:06:25.196
"There!" exclaimed Nemo as a passage, black and deep, finally opened before us.

69
00:06:25.197 --> 00:06:30.434
The captain took the wheel and I heard the waters of the Red Sea roaring through the tunnel.

70
00:06:30.435 --> 00:06:36.275
On the walls of the narrow passage, I could see nothing but bright streaks from our electric light.

71
00:06:36.276 --> 00:06:44.918
Our engines churned the waves and the Nautilus went with the torrent, as swift as an arrow. My heart pounded furiously.

72
00:06:44.919 --> 00:06:49.942
At last Captain Nemo handed the wheel back to the pilot and turned to me.

73
00:06:49.943 --> 00:06:54.894
"The Mediterranean!" he announced triumphantly.
